reasoning:
We need to analyze the problem.

The test scenario:

Frozen journey requirements:
Step 0: seek #shop (likely focusing the shop button)
Step 1: press Enter -> expects aria-expanded="true" on #shop and visible #plants (dropdown opens)
Step 2: seek #search (tab out of navigation to search input) -> expects aria-expanded="false" on #shop and #plants invisible

Observed failure: At step 2, after tabbing to #search, the assertions fail because aria-expanded on #shop is still "true" (observed true expected false) and #plants visible (still true).

Thus pressing Tab away from the shop button does not close the dropdown. Expected behavior: When focus leaves the dropdown (or the menu), the dropdown should close.

In many accessible menus, pressing Tab to move focus out of the menu should close it. In the code, likely missing a blur handler that closes the menu when focus leaves the menu items.

Looking at the source: We have a DisclosureMenu class extending BaseMenu. It handles focus via _handleFocus: each menu item link gets a focus listener that sets focusState=self and currentChild=index.

But there is no handling for when focus leaves the menu (blur) to close it. Possibly need to add a blur listener on the menu or on the controller to close when focus goes outside.

Specifically, the test expects that after tabbing from #shop to #search (which is outside the nav), the dropdown closes. Currently, focus goes from #shop (button) to #plants? Actually the observed focusEvents sequence shows:

From body -> #home (sequence 0)
#home -> #contact (1)
#contact -> #shop (2)
#shop -> #plants (3)   <-- after pressing Enter on shop, focus moves to first link? Actually after Enter, the dropdown opens and focus may go to first item? The observed focusEvents shows from #shop to #plants (so after opening, focus moved to Plants link).
Then #plants -> #tools (4)
#tools -> #search (5)

So after opening, focus is on Plants link. Then tab moves to Tools, then tab to Search (outside nav). The expectation is that when focus leaves the nav (i.e., when focus goes to #search), the dropdown should close.

Thus we need to detect when focus leaves the menu (or when focusState changes to none?) and close the dropdown.

In BaseMenu, there is a blur() method that sets focusState="none" and blurs the menu if shouldFocus. There's also blurChildren() that blurs all menu items and submenus.

We could add a listener on the menu for "focusout" (or blur) to close the menu when focus leaves entirely.

Alternatively, we could add a listener on the controller (the shop button) for blur to close if menu is open and relatedTarget not inside menu.

Simpler: In DisclosureMenu's _handleFocus, we could also add a blur listener on each menu item? But we need to detect when focus leaves the whole menu.

Better: Add a listener on the menu element for "focusout" event, check if the relatedTarget is outside the menu, then close.

Let's look at BaseMenu's _handleFocus method:

_handleFocus() {
    this.elements.menuItems.forEach((menuItem, index) => {
      menuItem.dom.link.addEventListener("focus", () => {
        this.focusState = "self";
        this.currentChild = index;
      });
    });
  }

We could also add a blur listener to each link to set focusState="none"? But that would fire when moving between items inside menu, causing unwanted closures.

Instead, we can add a focusout listener on the menu itself.

Add in _handleFocus (or maybe a separate method) something like:

this.dom.menu.addEventListener("focusout", (event) => {
  if (!this.dom.menu.contains(event.relatedTarget)) {
    this.blur();
    this.closeChildren();
    if (this.elements.controller) {
      this.elements.controller.close();
    }
  }
});

But need to ensure we only close if it's a disclosure menu (has controller). For top-level menu, controller is the shop button.

We need to edit the DisclosureMenu class (since that's what is instantiated). However, the base class BaseMenu might be better to edit, but we must stick to allowed paths and hashes. The editable files include src/disclosureMenu.js, src/_baseMenu.js, etc. We can edit either.

